 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Normalization:** The weights must strictly sum to 1 to ensure $p_N(n)$ is a valid probability density function [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** The fitting process (§7.1) must handle potential numerical instability when lobes become nearly identical or weights approach zero [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Convolution Approximation:** When convolving $p_N(n)$ with an isotropic spherical distribution $g(\omega; n)$, the result is the sum of the convolution between each lobe $D_{i\text{sggx}}(n)$ and $g(\omega; n)$ [S1, unofficial]. Each per-lobe convolution is approximated as a similar but roughened SGGX distribution: $\int g(\omega; n) D_{\text{sggx}}(n; R, \alpha) \, dn \approx D_{\text{sggx}}(\omega; R, \alpha')$ [S1, unofficial].
 
